Fillets and chamfers whose contact runs along the far edge of a face (a
face exactly as wide as the contact distance, 0014): the ends of that
contact at the vertices of the edge (mitcad#121).

ChFi3d_Builder::SplitKPart: 0014 hatches such a tangency line a little
inside its face and puts the ends it finds on the arcs across, near a
vertex, at that vertex (SnapToVertex). That was done only where the
domains of both faces' lines are filled in the main loop. Where the other
line's domain is shorter at an end of the spine (the faces at the end are
slanted or curved, so that the contact on the other face reaches them
first), the end of the line on the boundary is filled later, in the
processing of the spine's extensions (intf, intl), without the snap. Its
common point then lay on the arc a hatching shift (ten times the 2d
tolerance) from the vertex: the arc was cut there, which left an edge of
that length in the face at the end, and the result was not a valid shell
(an inconsistently oriented face). Those calls now pass the snap tolerance
too.
